.docname {Notification Service Architecture}
.include {docs}

**Bounded context:** [Alerting](../domain/alerting.qd) · **Stack:** Rust / Axum · **Code-level walkthrough:** [Notification Service](../services/notification.qd)

The notification-service realises the [Alerting](../domain/alerting.qd) context. Architecturally it is a **rule engine and dispatcher**: it decides whether an event warrants an alert and fans it out to the right people across more than one channel.

---

## Architectural Style

**Ports & Adapters** — driving adapters → use cases → ports → driven adapters — organised around two responsibilities that map directly onto the context's two aggregates: managing *where* a principal can be reached, and *what* they have chosen to receive.

The service has **two driving adapters into the same use cases**, not just one. HTTP requests arrive through `adapters/driving/http_api`; temperature alerts also arrive through `adapters/driving/alert_listener.rs`, a Redis subscriber on `alerts:temperature` — a driving path with no controller in front of it, since there's no HTTP request to adapt. Both reach the same cooldown/domain-resolution/dual-delivery logic in `service/alerts.rs`.

.mermaid
    graph TD
        subgraph "Driving adapters"
            HTTP["http_api/controllers.rs\nsubscribe · preferences · trigger · push/temperature"]
            EL["alert_listener.rs\nRedis subscriber: alerts:temperature"]
        end
        subgraph "Use cases — service/"
            ALERTS["alerts.rs\ndebounce · resolve domains · dual delivery"]
            PUSH["push.rs\nrecipient resolution · fan-out · cleanup"]
            PREFS["preferences.rs\ndevice registration · opt-in writes"]
        end
        subgraph "Ports — service/ports.rs"
            P["NotificationBus · Cooldown · DomainDirectory\nSubscriptionStore · PreferenceStore · PushSender · Clock"]
        end
        HTTP --> ALERTS
        HTTP --> PREFS
        EL --> ALERTS
        ALERTS --> PUSH
        ALERTS --> P
        PUSH --> P
        PREFS --> P
        P --> TW[twin-service: resolve domains]
        P -.-> DB[(notification-db)]
        P -.->|publish + cooldown| BR[(broker)]

`POST /trigger`, `POST /push/temperature`, and the `alerts:temperature` Redis subscription — the path that actually fires for every automatic sensor-driven threshold breach (see [Communication & Data Flow](data-flow.qd)'s Threshold Alert flow) — all resolve recipients via twin-service, debounce, and dispatch on both channels. The two HTTP routes forward the calling user's own `x-gateway-claims` header to twin-service's building→domain lookup; the event-listener path has no HTTP caller to forward a token from, so it presents a fixed system identity instead (`system_claims_header()` in `domain/identity.rs`) — safe only because that specific twin-service route (`GET /domain/:building_name`) doesn't perform any per-identity authorization, it just requires *a* structurally valid claims header to exist.

Because twin's answer is unscoped, notification-service scopes it itself: the resolved domains are intersected with the caller's own `memberships` before anything is published or pushed. See [Notification Service](../services/notification.qd)'s membership table.

`POST /trigger` has a third caller besides the browser: twin-service itself, on a failed building provisioning (`Provisioning::fail`, see [Digital Twin Service](../services/digital-twin.qd)). Same shape as the event-listener path — no end user in the loop, so it authenticates as a fixed system identity too — but it's an in-mesh service-to-service call, not a Redis event.

.box {System callers bypass the membership filter} type:{note}
    An in-mesh caller has no memberships, so intersecting would drop every domain and silently kill twin-service's provisioning-failure alert — which never inspects the response status. `Audience::of` therefore returns `Unrestricted` for any subject prefixed `system:`, and `Domains(...)` for everyone else. The prefix is only as trustworthy as the mesh perimeter: the edge strips client-supplied `x-gateway-claims` and re-injects verified claims, so an external caller cannot mint one, while any in-mesh workload can — the same accepted trust boundary described in [Service Mesh Architecture](service-mesh.qd).

.box {The event-listener path degrades gracefully instead of failing loudly} type:{note}
    `push_temperature` returns a `Validation` error (400) if no domain can be determined — reasonable for a synchronous HTTP call, where silence would be bad UX. The event-listener path can't do that (there's no HTTP response to fail), so if the twin-service lookup errors or resolves to zero domains, it falls back to an unscoped broadcast straight to the `notifications` channel rather than dropping the alert entirely. The 5-minute cooldown still applies either way, so a building that can't be resolved doesn't flood the channel.

### Components & Connectors diagram

![Notification Service C&C diagram](images/cc-notification-service.svg)

---

## Key Architectural Decisions

- **Two responsibilities, two models.** Delivery channels and alert preferences are kept apart so a device can be revoked without losing choices, and choices can change without re-registering devices.
- **Dual-channel delivery, on every temperature-alert path.** `trigger`, `push_temperature`, and the automatic `alerts:temperature` listener all dispatch both an in-app event (through the broker) and a push to offline devices, so reachability does not depend on the app being open.
- **Repeat suppression, on every temperature-alert path.** A 5-minute Redis-backed cooldown, keyed per building and room (`temp_alert:{buildingId}:{roomId}`), stops the same condition from re-alerting on every reading — including the automatic sensor-driven path, which is the one under the most repeat-alert pressure (a stuck sensor reading the same breach every tick).
- **Recipient resolution by collaboration.** Where a path resolves recipients at all, it asks twin-service which domains a building belongs to and translates that into recipients, rather than keeping its own copy of building structure (an anti-corruption boundary).
- **Self-pruning channels.** A delivery channel that the provider reports as permanently dead (`410`/`403`) is removed automatically on the next push attempt, keeping the model clean without a scheduled job.

---

## Integration

- Owns `notification-db`; no other service reads it.
- Receives temperature events from sensor-service via the `alerts:temperature` Redis channel; publishes to the `notifications` channel for socket-service to relay — see [Communication & Data Flow](data-flow.qd).
- Calls twin-service to resolve a building's domains — from the authenticated HTTP alert routes (`trigger`, `push_temperature`, forwarding the caller's own claims, then intersecting the answer with their memberships) and from the `alerts:temperature` event listener (using a fixed system identity, since there's no caller to forward).
- Called by twin-service on a failed building provisioning (`POST /trigger`, system identity, in-mesh) — see [Digital Twin Service](../services/digital-twin.qd).

For the throttle mechanics, VAPID delivery, the cleanup behaviour, and the API, see the [Notification Service](../services/notification.qd) internals page.
